/div><div class='wsp-wrap'><p><em>Last updated November 14, 2026.</em></p><p><strong>North Carolina law sets out when a person occupying land can claim title by adverse possession, and how an owner can remove someone who has no right to be there.</strong></p><h2>What the official sources say</h2><ul><li>G.S. 1-38(a): 7 years&#x27; possession under known and visible lines and boundaries AND under color of title bars actions by others; commissioner&#x27;s deeds in judicial sales and trustee&#x27;s foreclosure deeds also constitute color of title. (N.C.G.S. 1-38; <a href='https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/HTML/BySection/Chapter_1/GS_1-38.html' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li><li>G.S. 1-38(b): listing and paying taxes on property with marked boundaries and a recorded certified survey map is prima facie evidence of possession under known and visible lines and boundaries. (N.C.G.S. 1-38(b); <a href='https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/HTML/BySection/Chapter_1/GS_1-38.html' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li></ul><div class=\"chb-cta\" style=\"margin:32px 0;border-radius:10px;background:linear-gradient(120deg,#0F1C2E 0%,#16293F 100%);padding:28px 32px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:20px;flex-wrap:wrap;color:#fff;\"><div style=\"max-width:600px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:2px;color:#E0A83C;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:6px;\">Selling in North Carolina?</div><div style=\"font-weight:800;font-size:20px;line-height:1.35;color:#fff;\">We buy North Carolina houses as-is, with no commission, no repairs and no closing costs to you.</div></div><a href=\"#\" onclick=\"var f=document.querySelector('iframe[data-form-id]');if(f){f.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth',block:'center'});return false;}\" style=\"flex-shrink:0;padding:14px 24px;border-radius:8px;background:#E0A83C;color:#16203A;font-size:14px;font-weight:800;text-decoration:none;white-space:nowrap;\">Get My Cash Offer &rarr;</a></div><h2>North Carolina Squatters Rights and Adverse Possession FAQ</h2><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Can a squatter take ownership of my North Carolina house?</h3><p> Check the statute and talk to a North Carolina attorney about your facts.</p><div class='wsp-related'><div class='wsp-related-grid'><div class='wsp-related-card'><a href='https://cashhomebuyersca.com/north-carolina-landlord-tenant-law-tenant-rights/'>North Carolina Landlord Tenant Law and Tenant Rights</a><p>Official sources.</p></div><div class='wsp-related-card'><a href='https://cashhomebuyersca.com/north-carolina-eviction-notice/'>North Carolina Eviction Notice: Types and Days</a><p>Official sources.</p></div><div class='wsp-related-card'><a href='https://cashhomebuyersca.com/north-carolina-tax-deed-and-tax-lien-sales/'>North Carolina Tax Deed and Tax Lien Sales</a><p>Official sources.</p></div><div class='wsp-related-card'><a href='https://cashhomebuyersca.com/north-carolina-guides-by-city-and-county/'>North Carolina guides by city and county</a><p>Every local guide we publish.</p></div><div class='wsp-related-card'><a href='https://cashhomebuyersca.com/we-buy-houses-in-north-carolina/'>We buy houses in North Carolina</a><p>How a cash offer works.</p></div></div></div><p><em>General information for North Carolina Squatters Rights and Adverse Possession, not legal or tax advice. Confirm details with the official office or a licensed professional.</em></p><script type=\"application/ld+json\">{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\", \"@type\": \"FAQPage\", \"mainEntity\": [{\"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"Can a squatter take ownership of my North Carolina house?\", \"acceptedAnswer\": {\"@type\": \"Answer\", \"text\": \"Only by meeting every requirement of the adverse possession statute cited above for the full period. Check the statute and talk to a North Carolina attorney about your facts.\"}}]}</script><script type=\"application/ld+json\">{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\", \"@type\": \"BreadcrumbList\", \"itemListElement\": [{\"@type\": \"ListItem\", \"position\": 1, \"name\": \"Home\", \"item\": \"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/\"}, {\"@type\": \"ListItem\", \"position\": 2, \"name\": \"North Carolina\", \"item\": \"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/we-buy-houses-in-north-carolina/\"}, {\"@type\": \"ListItem\", \"position\": 3, \"name\": \"North Carolina Squatters Rights and Adverse Possession\", \"item\": \"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/north-carolina-squatters-rights-adverse-possession/\"}]}</script></div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->", "status": "publish", "rank_math_title": "North Carolina Squatters Rights and Adverse Possession", "rank_math_description": "North Carolina squatters rights and adverse possession: what the statutes say and how owners remove occupants or sell. Official sources.", "rank_math_focus_keyword": "north carolina squatters rights"}, {"slug": "north-carolina-landlord-tenant-law-tenant-rights", "title": "North Carolina Landlord Tenant Law and Tenant Rights: Rules for Selling a Rental", "content": "<!-- wp:html -->\n<style>.wsp-hero{position:relative;margin:0 calc(-50vw + 50%);padding:5rem 1.5rem;background-image:none;background:linear-gradient(170deg,#12293f 0%,#0c1c2e 46%,#060c16 100%);overflow:hidden;}.wsp-hero-inner{max-width:1216px;margin:0 auto;display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1fr;gap:2.5rem;align-items:center;}.wsp-hero-left{text-align:center;}.wsp-hero-left h1{color:#fff;font-size:2.6rem;font-weight:800;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0 1rem;text-shadow:1px 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.3);}.wsp-hero-left h2{color:#fff;font-weight:300;font-size:1.3rem;margin:0 0 1rem;text-shadow:1px 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.3);}.wsp-hero-left p{color:#fff;font-size:1rem;font-weight:300;max-width:500px;margin:0 auto 1.5rem;text-shadow:1px 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.3);}.wsp-btn-outline{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;border:2px solid #fff;border-radius:30px;padding:.6rem 1.4rem;font-weight:600;color:#fff;text-decoration:none;font-size:.95rem;}.wsp-btn-outline:hover{background:#fff;color:#1A202C;}.wsp-hero-right iframe{width:100%;min-height:560px;border:none;border-radius:4px;}.wsp-wrap{max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:3rem 1.5rem 4rem;}@media (max-width:900px){.wsp-hero-inner{grid-template-columns:1fr;}.wsp-hero-left h1{font-size:2.1rem;}}.wsp-wrap h3{background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:4px solid #3182CE;border-radius:8px;padding:.9rem 1.25rem;margin:1.75rem 0 0;font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;color:#1A202C;}.wsp-wrap h3+p{background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-top:none;border-radius:0 0 8px 8px;padding:1rem 1.25rem;margin:0 0 .25rem;color:#444;}.wsp-wrap table td{vertical-align:top;}.chb-cta p{margin:0}.wsp-related{margin:2.5rem 0 1rem}.wsp-related h2{font-size:1.3rem;margin-bottom:1rem;font-weight:600;}.wsp-related-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(220px,1fr));gap:1rem;}.wsp-related-card{background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;}.wsp-related-card a{font-weight:700;color:var(--global-palette1, #3182CE);text-decoration:none;}.wsp-related-card a:hover{text-decoration:underline;}.wsp-related-card p{margin:.35rem 0 0;font-size:.9rem;color:#555;}.wsp-hero a.wsp-btn-call{background:#E0A83C !important;border:2px solid #E0A83C !important;color:#16203A !important;font-size:1.25rem !important;font-weight:800 !important;padding:1rem 2.2rem !important;border-radius:40px !important;box-shadow:0 0 0 4px rgba(224,168,60,.25),0 8px 26px rgba(224,168,60,.5) !important;text-shadow:none !important;}.wsp-hero a.wsp-btn-call strong{color:#16203A !important;}.wsp-hero a.wsp-btn-call:hover{background:#fff !important;border-color:#fff !important;}</style><div class='wsp-hero'><div class='wsp-hero-inner'><div class='wsp-hero-left'><h1>North Carolina Landlord Tenant Law and Tenant Rights: Rules for Selling a Rental</h1><div class=wsp-kicker>Cash Home Buyers in North Carolina</div><div class=wsp-speed><span>Cash Offer in 24 Hours</span></div><ul class=wsp-checks><li>North Carolina Landlord Tenant Law and Tenant Rights: inherited, behind on payments, tenants or repairs, we buy the house as-is</li><li>No fees, commissions or closing costs on a North Carolina Landlord Tenant Law and Tenant Rights sale</li><li>Turn the North Carolina Landlord Tenant Law and Tenant Rights offer down and you owe us nothing</li></ul><h2>Selling a North Carolina House? Get a Cash Offer</h2><p>We buy as-is.</p><p><a class='wsp-btn-outline wsp-btn-call' href='tel:424-435-2326'>Call or Text <strong>&nbsp;(424) 435-2326</strong></a></p></div><div class='wsp-hero-right'><iframe src='https://connect.cashhomebuyersca.com/widget/form/zKzKbSeB3aqwWwE8NNxO' data-form-id='zKzKbSeB3aqwWwE8NNxO' title='Cash Offer Form'></iframe><br /><script src='https://connect.cashhomebuyersca.com/js/form_embed.js' async></script></div></div></div><div class='wsp-wrap'><p><em>Last updated November 14, 2026.</em></p><p><strong>North Carolina statutes set the basic rules between landlords and tenants, including deposits, notices and what happens when a rental is sold.</strong></p><h2>What the official sources say</h2><ul><li>G.S. 42-51(b): security deposit may not exceed 2 weeks&#x27; rent (week-to-week), 1.5 months&#x27; rent (month-to-month), or 2 months&#x27; rent (terms longer than month-to-month). (N.C.G.S. 42-51(b); <a href='https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/HTML/BySection/Chapter_42/GS_42-51.html' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li><li>G.S. 42-52: landlord must itemize damage and mail/deliver it with the balance of the deposit within 30 days after termination and delivery of possession; if the claim cannot be determined, interim accounting within 30 days and final accounting within 60 days; no withholding for normal wear and tear. (N.C.G.S. 42-52; <a href='https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/HTML/BySection/Chapter_42/GS_42-52.html' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li><li>G.S. 42-14: month-to-month tenancy ends by a notice to quit of 7 days; week-to-week 2 days; year-to-year one month; manufactured-home space rentals 60 days. (N.C.G.S. 42-14; <a href='https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/HTML/BySection/Chapter_42/GS_42-14.html' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li><li>G.S. 42-42(a): landlord must comply with housing codes, make repairs to keep premises fit and habitable, keep common areas safe, maintain electrical/plumbing/heating etc. (after written tenant notice except emergencies), and provide operable smoke and carbon monoxide alarms. (N.C.G.S. 42-42(a); <a href='https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/HTML/BySection/Chapter_42/GS_42-42.html' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li><li>G.S. 42-37.1(a): state public policy protects tenants who seek to exercise rights to decent, safe, and sanitary housing (retaliatory eviction provision; remaining subsections not read). (N.C.G.S. 42-37.1(a); <a href='https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/HTML/BySection/Chapter_42/GS_42-37.1.html' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li></ul><div class=\"chb-cta\" style=\"margin:32px 0;border-radius:10px;background:linear-gradient(120deg,#0F1C2E 0%,#16293F 100%);padding:28px 32px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:20px;flex-wrap:wrap;color:#fff;\"><div style=\"max-width:600px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:2px;color:#E0A83C;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:6px;\">Selling in North Carolina?</div><div style=\"font-weight:800;font-size:20px;line-height:1.35;color:#fff;\">We buy North Carolina houses as-is, with no commission, no repairs and no closing costs to you.</div></div><a href=\"#\" onclick=\"var f=document.querySelector('iframe[data-form-id]');if(f){f.scrollIntoView({behavior: