435-2326</strong></a></p></div><div class='wsp-hero-right'><iframe src='https://connect.cashhomebuyersca.com/widget/form/zKzKbSeB3aqwWwE8NNxO' data-form-id='zKzKbSeB3aqwWwE8NNxO' title='Cash Offer Form'></iframe><br /><script src='https://connect.cashhomebuyersca.com/js/form_embed.js' async></script></div></div></div><div class='wsp-wrap'><p><em>Last updated November 14, 2026.</em></p><p><strong>North Carolina law requires a landlord to give a specific written notice before filing an eviction, and the number of days depends on the reason.</strong></p><h2>What the official sources say</h2><ul><li>G.S. 42-3: for leases with a fixed rent payment time, the term is forfeited if rent is not paid within 10 days after demand for all past-due rent; lessor may then enter and dispossess. (N.C.G.S. 42-3; <a href='https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/HTML/BySection/Chapter_42/GS_42-3.html' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li><li>G.S. 42-26(a): summary ejectment lies against a tenant who holds over after the term expires, after demand for surrender, or who breaches a lease condition causing the estate to cease, or who deserts premises in arrears. (N.C.G.S. 42-26; <a href='https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/HTML/BySection/Chapter_42/GS_42-26.html' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li><li>G.S. 42-14: month-to-month tenancy ended by 7 days&#x27; notice to quit (no-cause). (N.C.G.S. 42-14; <a href='https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/HTML/BySection/Chapter_42/GS_42-14.html' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li></ul><div class=\"chb-cta\" style=\"margin:32px 0;border-radius:10px;background:linear-gradient(120deg,#0F1C2E 0%,#16293F 100%);padding:28px 32px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:20px;flex-wrap:wrap;color:#fff;\"><div style=\"max-width:600px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:2px;color:#E